        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill Deck Replacement: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A treadmill deck wears out, and when it does the belt drags, slips and runs hot
            no matter how it is lubricated or tensioned.</strong> On many home machines the deck is
            reversible: it can be unbolted, turned over and refitted once, which gives a fresh
            running surface for the cost of an afternoon and a bottle of lubricant. If it has
            already been flipped, or if the design is single-sided, the board is replaced.
          </p>
          <p>
            A replacement deck as a part commonly costs somewhere in the region of $100 to $300 for
            a typical home treadmill, and more for long or commercial-grade boards. Fitted by a
            technician, and usually done together with a new belt because the two wear together,
            owners commonly report totals in the region of $300 to $600 or more. A flip, by
            contrast, costs only labour, and that labour is yours if you do it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any of that, confirm the deck is actually the problem. The symptoms overlap with
            a dry deck and a worn belt, and both are cheaper to fix. The next two sections explain
            what the deck is and how to tell it has worn, and the rest of the page walks through
            the flip, the replacement, the cost and the decision about whether the machine is worth
            it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-the-deck-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Deck Is and Why It Wears</h2>
<p>
            The deck is the rigid board that sits between the front and rear rollers, inside the
            loop of the walking belt. The top run of the belt slides across it, and every footstrike
            presses the belt down onto it. It is the thing that makes a treadmill feel solid
            underfoot, and it is also the single largest source of friction in the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            On most home treadmills the deck is a sheet of medium-density fibreboard or plywood,
            typically somewhere in the region of three-quarters of an inch to an inch thick, with a
            hard, low-friction surface on top. That surface is usually a phenolic resin laminate, a
            similar high-pressure laminate, or on cheaper machines a wax or lacquer coating applied
            to the board. Better decks are laminated on both faces so they can be reversed; cheaper
            ones are coated on one side only. The deck is bolted to the frame, often through rubber
            or elastomer cushions that provide the machine's shock absorption, which is why our
            guide to <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill cushioning</a>
            spends so long on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            It wears because the belt's underside and the deck's surface are in constant sliding
            contact under your weight, separated only by a thin film of silicone lubricant. That
            film does most of the work. When it thins or disappears the belt and deck rub directly,
            friction climbs, both surfaces heat, and the hard laminate begins to wear through in
            the zone where your feet land. The wear is rarely even: it concentrates in a band a
            few feet from the front, where most people strike, and often favours one side if the
            belt has been tracking off-centre.
          </p>
          <p>
            The main ways a deck fails, in order of how often technicians see them:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The surface wears through.</strong> The glossy laminate dulls, then abrades
            down to the fibreboard beneath in the foot-strike zone. Once the board is exposed it
            soaks up lubricant and wears fast.</li>
            <li><strong>Grooves and dips.</strong> Channels worn by the belt's edges or by a belt
            running off-centre, and a shallow depression where the feet land.</li>
            <li><strong>Soft spots.</strong> Areas where the fibreboard has lost its integrity,
            usually after the surface has gone, and sometimes from moisture.</li>
            <li><strong>Delamination.</strong> The laminate lifting from the board, or the board's
            own layers separating. Sweat dripping onto the belt and running under it, humidity in a
            garage or basement, and spilled drinks are the common causes.</li>
            <li><strong>Cracking.</strong> Less common, and usually from a board that has gone soft
            and then taken a heavy impact.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Lubrication is the whole story of deck life. A deck that is kept lubricated on schedule
            can outlast several belts; one run dry will take the belt with it in a fraction of the
            time. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt lubrication</a>
            is the maintenance task this page exists to make unnecessary.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="symptoms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Symptoms of a Worn Treadmill Deck</h2>
<p>
            The frustrating thing about a worn deck is that every symptom looks like something else
            first. Work through them with that in mind.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt drags or slows underfoot even after lubrication and correct tension.</strong>
            This is the defining symptom. A belt that hesitates when you push off, or slows
            momentarily under each step, is usually fixed by lubricating a dry deck or tightening a
            loose belt. If you have done both properly and the hesitation returns within days, the
            friction is coming from a surface that lubricant can no longer protect. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt that slows down when you step on it</a>
            walks the full order of checks, and the deck is the last stop on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubricant disappears almost immediately.</strong> On a healthy deck a proper
            application lasts weeks or months. If you apply it and the underside of the belt is dry
            again after a couple of sessions, either the belt's underside has glazed and will not
            hold it, or the deck surface has worn to bare board and is absorbing it. Lift the belt
            edge with the machine unplugged and look at both.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dip or a step you can feel.</strong> Walk slowly on the stopped belt in socks
            and pay attention to the foot-strike zone. A shallow depression, a ridge at its edge, or
            a spot that feels softer than the rest of the deck is wear in the board, not the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt wears on one side.</strong> A belt that frays or glazes on one edge
            only, and that keeps drifting back to that side after centring, is often following a
            groove or a low spot in the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor runs hot, or the breaker trips under load.</strong> Friction at the
            deck is load on the motor. A motor that is noticeably hotter than it used to be after a
            session, a hot-electrics smell from the hood, or a breaker that holds at walking pace
            and trips when you run, can all be a worn deck making the motor work far harder than it
            was designed to. A burning smell is a stop-now symptom regardless of cause.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Visible wear with the belt lifted.</strong> This is the confirmation. With the
            machine unplugged, loosen the belt enough to lift one edge well clear, shine a torch
            across the deck surface at a low angle, and run a hand over it. You are looking for a
            dull band where the gloss has gone, a visible change in colour where the laminate has
            worn to board, grooves, ridges, lifted laminate at the edges or bubbles in it, and any
            area that gives under a thumb. A deck that is uniformly glossy and smooth is not your
            problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rule-out" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Rule Out the Cheaper Causes First</h2>
<p>
            A deck flip is an afternoon and a replacement is a real expense, so be sure. Three
            cheaper problems produce most of the same symptoms, and all three should be ruled out
            before the deck is blamed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A dry deck.</strong> If the belt's underside feels dry when you slide a hand
            under it, lubricate properly — the right lubricant, the right amount, applied under the
            belt across the deck's width, then run at walking pace for a few minutes to spread it.
            Give it a few sessions. If the symptoms are gone and stay gone, the deck was fine and
            you were one maintenance task behind.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A loose or off-centre belt.</strong> Tension and tracking are adjusted at the
            rear roller. A belt that slips only on push-off is usually loose; one that rubs a rail
            needs centring. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-adjust-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjusting a treadmill belt</a>
            covers both. Do not over-tighten in the attempt — a tight belt on a worn deck is the
            fastest way to overheat a motor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A worn belt.</strong> If the belt's underside is glazed smooth, its edges are
            fraying or its seam is lifting, the belt needs replacing whatever the deck is doing.
            The two are usually inspected together, and very often replaced or refreshed together,
            because fitting a new belt to a worn deck ruins the new belt and fitting a worn belt to
            a fresh deck scores the new surface. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt replacement cost</a>
            covers the belt side of that decision.
          </p>
          <p>
            Only when the belt is lubricated, correctly tensioned, centred and in reasonable
            condition, and the drag is still there, is the deck the answer. At that point lift the
            belt and look, as described above. The deck will usually make the case for itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flip-option" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Can the Deck Be Flipped?</h2>
<p>
            A reversible deck is laminated on both faces and mounted so that it can be removed,
            turned over end to end or side to side as the manual specifies, and bolted back in with
            the unused face up. It is one of the more satisfying repairs in treadmill ownership,
            because it costs nothing in parts and restores the machine to something close to new
            underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to tell whether yours is reversible.</strong> The manual is the authority.
            Many manufacturers state plainly that the deck is reversible and describe the
            procedure; some say nothing, which usually means it is not designed to be. If the
            manual is silent, there are three things to check with the machine unplugged:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Both faces finished.</strong> Lift the belt edge and look at the underside
            of the deck from the side or from below. A reversible deck has the same hard glossy
            laminate on the bottom as the top. A bare, rough or painted underside is single-sided.</li>
            <li><strong>Symmetrical mounting holes.</strong> If the bolt holes are drilled so that
            the board fits the frame either way up, it was designed to be flipped. Holes that are
            offset, countersunk on one side only, or accompanied by one-sided fixings for the
            cushions suggest otherwise.</li>
            <li><strong>No one-sided hardware.</strong> Some decks carry a belt guide, a sensor
            bracket or a lubrication port on one face. These generally cannot be turned over.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>How to tell whether it has already been flipped.</strong> On a second-hand
            machine, or one a technician has serviced before, the deck may have had its one flip.
            The giveaway is wear on both faces: the underside shows the same dull band and
            scoring as the top. A deck worn on both sides is a deck for the bin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When a flip is not enough.</strong> A deck that is soft, delaminated, swollen
            by moisture or cracked should not be flipped, because the board's structure is gone and
            the fresh face will not be flat or will not stay that way. The same applies to a deck
            where the wear is a deep groove rather than surface abrasion, since a groove on the old
            face means a belt that has been running off-centre for a long time, and that cause
            needs fixing too.
          </p>
          <p>
            Decks are generally flipped once, not repeatedly. Some owners have had a second
            lease out of a lightly worn deck by cleaning it thoroughly and lubricating heavily, but
            that is a delay rather than a repair.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flip-procedure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How a Deck Flip Is Done</h2>
<p>
            What follows is the general shape of the job, so that you can judge whether to take it
            on and know what a technician is doing when you pay for it. It is not a substitute for
            your manual, which sets the order of disassembly, the bolt locations and the torque for
            your model. Where this page and the manual differ, the manual wins.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Unplug the machine and remove the safety key.</strong> Lower the incline
            fully first if the deck sits on an incline frame, while it is still powered, then
            unplug.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the motor hood.</strong> Usually a handful of screws. Take the
            chance to vacuum the motor compartment while it is open.</li>
            <li><strong>Slacken the belt.</strong> Back off the rear roller adjustment bolts evenly
            and fully, counting the turns so you can return to the same starting point. On many
            machines the rear roller comes out entirely.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the side rails and foot rails.</strong> These are bolted or screwed
            from underneath or from the ends. Keep the fasteners in labelled bags; they are rarely
            all the same length.</li>
            <li><strong>Free the front roller.</strong> Slacken or remove it so the belt is loose
            around the deck. The drive belt comes off the motor pulley at this point.</li>
            <li><strong>Unbolt the deck.</strong> Typically four to eight bolts through the deck
            into the frame or cushions, some reached from above with the belt pushed aside, some
            from below. This is where two people matter: the deck is heavy, long and awkward, and
            the belt is still wrapped around it.</li>
            <li><strong>Lift the deck out with the belt, and slide the belt off.</strong> Note the
            direction the belt was running and the direction of its seam, because it goes back the
            same way.</li>
            <li><strong>Clean everything.</strong> Vacuum and wipe the frame, the rollers and the
            inside of the belt. Inspect the rollers for bearing play and the drive belt for cracks
            while it is accessible.</li>
            <li><strong>Flip the deck as the manual directs</strong> — end over end or side to
            side — and refit it, checking it sits flat on every cushion. Snug the bolts, then
            tighten evenly.</li>
            <li><strong>Refit the belt, rollers and rails</strong>, then lubricate the fresh deck
            face under the belt as you would for a new belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Re-tension and re-track.</strong> Return the rear roller bolts to their
            starting point, plug in, run at walking pace with nobody on the belt, and centre it a
            quarter-turn at a time. Then set tension so it does not slip under push-off and no
            tighter. Expect to re-check tracking over the next few sessions.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Allow a full afternoon the first time. The dismantling is the same as for a belt
            replacement with the deck bolts added, which is why the two jobs are so often done
            together; our guide to <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to replace a treadmill belt</a>
            covers the belt stages in more detail and the snags that catch people out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. When the Deck Must Be Replaced</h2>
<p>
            If the deck is single-sided, already flipped, soft, swollen, delaminated or cracked,
            the answer is a new board. The procedure is the flip procedure with a different deck
            going back in, so the labour is the same; the difference is the part and the
            business of getting the right one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ordering the right deck.</strong> Decks are model-specific. Length, width,
            thickness, the bolt-hole pattern and the position of any cut-outs all have to match
            the frame and the cushions, and a board a quarter-inch too thick can change belt
            clearance at the hood and rails. Order from the manufacturer or an authorised parts
            supplier by model and serial number; the serial matters because some models changed
            deck dimensions between production runs. Generic decks cut to size exist, and are
            sometimes the only option for a discontinued machine, but they need careful measuring
            of the old board including every hole, and a supplier willing to confirm the laminate
            type.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Availability.</strong> Decks for mainstream models are usually obtainable for
            years after the model is discontinued. For older, obscure or budget brands they may
            not be, and that alone can decide the repair-or-replace question.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Replace the belt at the same time.</strong> A belt that has been running on a
            worn deck has almost always glazed its underside and will score the new surface. It is
            poor economy to put an old belt on a new deck, and most technicians will say so.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check the cushions.</strong> The elastomer blocks the deck sits on harden and
            crack with age. With the deck out, this is the only easy moment to replace them, and on
            a machine that has lost its bounce it is worth doing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Match the surface.</strong> If the old deck had a particular low-friction
            coating or a lubricant-free surface specified by the manufacturer, the replacement
            should too, and the lubrication regime should follow the manual for that surface
            rather than general advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. What Deck Replacement Costs</h2>
<p>
            Every figure here is a typical range gathered from what owners and technicians
            commonly report. Prices vary by brand, size, region and by whether the service is
            independent or manufacturer-authorised, and large or commercial-grade decks can sit
            above all of these.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A deck flip, DIY:</strong> effectively the cost of a bottle of silicone
            lubricant and your afternoon.</li>
            <li><strong>A deck flip by a technician:</strong> a call-out fee commonly in the region
            of $75 to $150 plus one to two hours of labour. Often quoted together with a belt.</li>
            <li><strong>A replacement deck, part only:</strong> commonly $100 to $300 for a typical
            home treadmill, more for long or heavy-duty boards and for some premium brands.</li>
            <li><strong>Deck and belt, fitted by a technician, all-in:</strong> owners commonly
            report somewhere in the region of $300 to $600 or more, including parts, labour,
            lubrication and tracking.</li>
            <li><strong>New cushions, if needed:</strong> a modest part, added while the deck is
            out.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>DIY or technician.</strong> The flip is a reasonable DIY job for an owner who
            is comfortable with hand tools, has a helper for the lifting, and will take the time to
            re-track the belt afterwards. A replacement is the same job with more at stake in the
            ordering. The arguments for a technician are a heavy non-folding machine, a machine
            still under a labour warranty, uncertainty about whether the deck is really the fault,
            and the simple value of having someone look at the rollers, drive belt and motor while
            it is all apart. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>
            covers how to find one and what to ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty.</strong> Decks are usually treated as wear items with short cover, but
            check. Some manufacturers cover the deck for longer than the belt, and premature deck
            wear on a young machine can point to a cushion, tracking or frame problem that is
            covered.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Is It Worth It on Your Machine?</h2>
<p>
            A deck flip is nearly always worth doing, because it costs so little and restores so
            much. A deck replacement is the point where the machine's age and value come into it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The rule of thumb</strong> most technicians use is that if a repair approaches
            half the price of a comparable new machine, or exceeds what the machine would fetch
            second-hand in working order, it needs a good reason. A technician's deck-and-belt job
            on a budget treadmill often fails that test; the same job on a mid-range or premium
            machine with a strong frame and a healthy motor usually passes it comfortably, because
            a deck every several years is simply part of owning one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look at the whole machine.</strong> A worn deck means the motor has been
            overloaded for a while. If the motor is also running hot, the roller bearings are
            noisy, the incline struggles or the console is unreliable, the deck is one item on a
            list, and the list is the thing to price. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>
            helps place a machine in its life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Parts availability decides some cases.</strong> If no deck exists for the
            model and a generic cut-to-size board is the only option, the risk goes up, and on an
            old machine that can tip the decision.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>DIY changes the maths.</strong> A flip you do yourself, or a replacement deck
            and belt you fit yourself, costs the parts alone, and that can make a repair sensible
            on a machine where a technician's bill would not be. Be honest about whether you will
            finish the job — a half-dismantled treadmill in the spare room is the worst outcome of
            all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Making the Next Deck Last</h2>
<p>
            Deck wear is almost entirely a function of lubrication, cleanliness and moisture. A
            deck that is looked after can outlast several belts and the motor besides.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lubricate on schedule, with the right product.</strong> Silicone treadmill
            lubricant, at the interval in your manual, checked by hand between times. Not
            household oils, not sprays not intended for treadmills. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to lubricate a treadmill</a>
            sets out a workable routine. If the manual says the deck is a lubricant-free design,
            follow it.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the belt centred and correctly tensioned.</strong> An off-centre belt
            wears a groove; a tight belt grinds the surface. Check both every few weeks.</li>
            <li><strong>Use a mat and keep the floor clean.</strong> Dust and carpet fibre find
            their way under the belt and act as an abrasive on the deck. A mat and a regular
            vacuum round the machine do more than they seem to.</li>
            <li><strong>Deal with sweat.</strong> Wipe the belt and rails after every session, and
            consider a towel over the console area if you sweat heavily. Sweat running under the
            belt is a slow route to a delaminated deck.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the room.</strong> A humid basement or an unheated garage swells
            fibreboard and lifts laminate. If the machine lives in one, a dehumidifier and a cover
            when it is not in use help.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep up the rest of the schedule.</strong> A deck rarely fails alone. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance guide</a>
            covers the full routine that keeps belt, deck, rollers and motor in step.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are buying rather than repairing, a reversible deck is a feature worth asking
            about. It is not usually on the spec sheet, but it is the difference between a cheap
            afternoon's maintenance and an expensive part a few years from now.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a treadmill deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The deck is the rigid board that sits inside the loop of the walking belt between the two rollers. The belt slides over it and your feet press the belt against it on every step. On most home machines it is a thick fibreboard or plywood panel with a hard, low-friction laminate or coating on top, mounted on rubber cushions that provide the shock absorption.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my treadmill deck is worn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The belt drags or slows underfoot even after proper lubrication and correct tension, lubricant seems to vanish within a session or two, you can feel a dip or soft spot in the foot-strike zone, the belt wears on one side, or the motor runs hotter than it used to. Confirm by unplugging, lifting the belt edge and looking for a dull, worn or grooved band in the deck surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you flip a treadmill deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Many home treadmills have a deck laminated on both faces with symmetrical mounting holes so it can be removed, turned over and refitted once. Check the manual first; if it is silent, look for a finished laminate on the underside and symmetrical bolt holes. A deck that is soft, delaminated, cracked or already worn on both faces cannot be flipped.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to replace a treadmill deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A replacement deck as a part commonly costs somewhere in the region of $100 to $300 for a typical home treadmill, with long or heavy-duty boards costing more. Fitted by a technician, usually together with a new belt because the two wear together, owners commonly report totals in the region of $300 to $600 or more. A flip costs only labour.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I replace the belt when I replace the deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always. A belt that has been running on a worn deck has usually glazed its underside and will score the fresh surface and drag on it. The dismantling is the same for both jobs, so doing them together saves labour. The same logic applies the other way: fitting a new belt to a worn deck ruins the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a treadmill deck last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed figure. A deck that is lubricated on schedule, kept clean and dry, and run with a centred, correctly tensioned belt can outlast several belts and many years of use. One run dry, in a damp room, or with a belt grinding against one rail can wear through in a fraction of that time. Lubrication is the deciding factor.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Decks</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most decks are destroyed by neglect rather than mileage, and most deck repairs go wrong at one of these four points.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the deck dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A thin film of silicone lubricant is all that separates the belt from the deck, and once it goes both surfaces wear fast and the motor labours. Lubricate at the interval in your manual, check the belt underside by hand between times, and use treadmill silicone rather than household oils or sprays.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fitting a new belt on a worn deck, or an old belt on a new one</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The two wear together and should be refreshed together. A new belt on a worn deck drags, glazes and slips within months; a glazed old belt on a fresh deck scores the surface. Inspect both when either is exposed, and do the belt at the same time as any flip or deck replacement.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flipping a deck that is soft or delaminated</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A flip only works when the board underneath is sound. A deck that is swollen by moisture, lifting at the edges, cracked or soft under a thumb will not sit flat and the fresh face will wear quickly. Check the structure before investing the afternoon, and replace the board if it fails.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening the belt to hide deck drag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Tension masks the symptom for a week and loads the motor, bearings and belt in the process, often ending in a hot motor or a tripped breaker. Set tension only to stop slip under push-off. If drag persists at correct tension on a lubricated deck, the deck is the problem and no amount of tightening fixes it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

